"""What the component does, as a rule a reader can follow.

Two situations, told apart by where a token sits in its document.

1. The first token of a document. The model resets its attention at each document
   start, so this token is read with no text before it, and the component's value is set
   by what the token is:
   - code keywords and code identifiers (int, package, strcmp, foo_bar) push it strongly down;
   - capitalised and lowercase English words push it a little down;
   - control tokens such as <|im_start|> push it down;
   - whitespace and ASCII punctuation push it up (tabs and newlines most), backslash
     escapes (\n, \t) push it up, and decorative runs (****, ////, ████) push it up further;
   - the other symbols and emoji push it a little down.
2. Any later token in a document. Here the component fires on
   - a middle-dot dot-leader token (··) as found in runs of dot leaders, a fixed high value;
   - comment and separator markers (//, #, ##, ###, /*, --, ..., …);
   - a code-block opener ("{" at the end of a line);
   - a minus sign that begins a value, after "=", ",", ":", "(", "is" or "between"
     (so "x = -3" fires but "5 - 3" does not), and a minus glued to such an opener (=-, (-).
Everything else is 0. The values are rough class averages, not fitted per token.
"""
import re

READS = ()

DOT_LEADER = "··"
MARKERS = {"//", "#", "##", "###", "/*", "--", "...", "…"}
BLOCK_OPENERS = {"{\n", " {\n"}
VALUE_OPENERS = {"=", ",", ":", "(", "is", "between", ")="}
CODE_WORDS = {
    "void", "int", "char", "uint", "long", "short", "float", "double", "bool", "return",
    "package", "import", "include", "class", "public", "private", "static", "const",
    "struct", "using", "namespace", "def", "self", "function", "var", "let", "print",
    "printf", "strcmp", "strlen", "memcpy", "malloc", "free", "throw", "try", "catch",
    "new", "this", "super", "null", "nil", "None", "true", "false", "switch", "break",
    "elif", "typedef", "sizeof", "unsigned", "cout", "cin", "ostream", "pthread",
    "fprintf", "snprintf", "yield", "lambda", "module", "export", "require",
}
